The character portraits' source
================================

A 64x64 portrait for every race and class, as a man and as a woman: 784 in
all, in early-1990s RPG pixel art and the DB32 palette.  An AI image
generator drew them in November 2026, sixteen to a sheet, to a brief written
for this game.  The brief, every sheet it returned and the reasons each
portrait was kept or drawn again are in archive/design_brief_portraits/,
which is not in the repository.

    source.png      every accepted portrait, cut to 64x64 and snapped to the
                    palette, on transparency.  This is the art.
    source.txt      which race and class is where in source.png
    backdrops.txt   each class's backdrop, the gradient a window sets its
                    portraits on
    recipe.txt      which take of each portrait was used: the brief's
                    accepted.txt, copied here
    fixes.txt       the clean-ups each was cut with, copied from the brief's
                    sheets

`build-portraits.py` reads source.png, source.txt and backdrops.txt and
writes lib/tcl/portraits/portraits.png (64), portraits-32.png (the same
shrunk, for the Character Record and the tombstone) and portraits.txt.
`build-portraits.py --cut` rewrites source.png, source.txt, recipe.txt and
fixes.txt from the brief's sheets, and needs the brief and ImageMagick.
`--review DIR` writes labelled sheets of all 784 at both sizes to look at.

A race or class added to lib/gamedata fails the build until it has a
portrait.  Until then the game shows the character's map tile in its place.

How a sheet was cut
-------------------

Each sheet is a 4x4 grid of cells on flat magenta.  A cell is split into
64x64 blocks; a block is transparent when less than half of it is figure,
and otherwise takes the median colour of its figure pixels, snapped to DB32
(less pink, which keys out against the magenta) and a few colours the
generator kept drawing that DB32 lacks (EXTRA in build-portraits.py).  Dark
grid lines a sheet drew between its cells are painted out first.

fixes.txt gives what one cell needed on top: `paint:` turns one colour into
another inside a rectangle (an eye that snapped to the wrong colour, a glow
drawn see-through), `clear:` empties a rectangle (a stray weapon), `accent:`
keeps a small mark such as a red eye that the median would lose, `green:`
boosts the green of a skin before the snap, and `add:` gives the snap extra
colours.  The brief's tools/cut.py is the tool every sheet was judged with
as it came back; it cuts with these same functions.
